      "utf8": "that has market pricing information updated \nthousands of times a second. We saw a version of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566400,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this with mortgage back securities in the leadup \nto 2008. Pension and retirement funds were quiet,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571920,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but also enormous holders of the tanches that blew \nup. More recently, it's been private credit and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577360,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "BDC's being aggressively marketed into target date \nfunds. And now after last year's executive order,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583680,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the big push is to make private equity and crypto \nproducts directly accessible to every 401k plan  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588880,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the country. The same trillion dollar private \ncredit industry that has started to run out of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593680,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "good borrowers looking at a $14 trillion pile of \ncaptive 401k money and seeing an exit door. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599920,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it all works because whoever gets to hold that \nmoney also gets to hide behind the phrase that's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604640,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "people's retirement when things start going wrong. \nNow, to play devil's advocate, this wouldn't  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609520,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "necessarily be a problem if people actually \npaid attention to where the retirement was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613520,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "being invested and were willing to accept those \nrisks. But this issue also needs to be considered  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 618480,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "alongside the other problems with treating \nretirement savings as an endangered species  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622560,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that must be protected at all costs. So, yeah, the \nsecond problem might not be that shocking to you,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628400,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but it still needs to be considered, and that is \nthat retirement savings held in these advantage  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633120,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accounts are overwhelmingly held by already \nvery wealthy households. According to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637840,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Federal Reserve's most recent survey of consumer \nfinances, the top 10% of American households hold  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 642960,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the overwhelming majority of all retirement \naccount balances. The top 1% on their own hold  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648160,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more in these accounts than the entire bottom \nhalf of the country combined. And to be fair,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652880,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that bottom half of households have on average \nbasically nothing in dedicated retirement accounts  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657920,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at all. The point is that when a politician says \nwe have to do X because that's people's retirement  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663440,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at stake, the retirement actually being defended \nbelongs overwhelmingly to people who are going  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668080,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be fine anyway. The phrase people's retirement \nsounds like it is defending a plumber 3 years away  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674080,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from clocking out for the last time. In practice, \nit is defending the ancillary portfolio of someone  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679440,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who statistically already has a healthy base \nof personal wealth. Now, for what it's worth,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684720,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this breakdown of retirement savings is actually a \nmore equitable distribution of wealth than direct  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689760,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stock ownership, which skews even harder to the \ntop. This is often used to highlight the power of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695200,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these accounts to make long-term investing more \nequitable across all workers in the economy.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this still has some problems. For one thing, \nbeing slightly more equitable than regular equity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705600,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holdings is not exactly a ringing endorsement. \nThe second thing is that the households with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710240,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a majority of these assets held in retirement \naccounts are largely the same households holding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714320,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a majority of directly held assets as well. So \nreally instead of bridging the gap, it's adding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719440,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "further distance between these percentiles. And \nfinally, well, you may be personally fine with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724640,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this kind of inequality of asset ownership. At \nthe end of the day, we do live in a theoretically  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730160,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "capitalist free market economy and some level \nof inequality is just inherent in that system.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735600,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the difference is with regular asset markets, \ntheir inequality isn't tax advantaged. So yeah,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741520,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problem number three is that because these are tax \nadvantaged accounts, every dollar going in is a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746320,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dollar that gets omitted from government revenue. \nAnd this is not a small issue. According to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751920,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "joint committee on taxation, retirement account \nrelated tax breaks cost the federal government  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756400,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "$383 billion in fiscal year 2025. This is a cost \nthat is much harder to see because it is emitted  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763360,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "revenue that just never comes in rather than \na budget line item that comes directly out of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767680,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Treasury. But to put that into context, $383 \nbillion is more than the federal government spends  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774000,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on SNAP, unemployment insurance, child nutrition, \nand federal family support programs combined with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780160,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough left over to fund NASA as a cherry on \ntop. To address this even more directly, it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 785680,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is also enough to just cut a $7,000 annual check \nto every single American over the age of 67. Now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 792080,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "obviously, that is not going to fund a retirement \nby itself, but it would be a serious top up to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796880,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Social Security for every senior regardless of \nwhether they had spent a career maxing out a 401k  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 801760,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or not. Now again to play devil's advocate here, \nthe argument is that these tax breaks encourage  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 807280,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "regular workers to save, which takes pressure \noff the governments to support them later,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811680,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which might actually be a decent argument if the \ntax benefit was spread evenly. But by combining  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818080,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problem two with problem three, it really isn't \nbecause the tax break scales with how much you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 823360,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can afford to contribute, and that scales with \nhow much you earn. The vast majority of that $383  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828800,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "billion flows straight to people who were going \nto be financially comfortable in retirement either  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833200,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way. Of course, there are extreme examples of this \nlike Peter Teal compounding billions of dollars  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838240,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "worth of Facebook stock in his tax advantage \nRoth IRA. But more broadly, the majority of these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843360,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "advantages go to people who just don't really need \nthem. It is a subsidy that gets sold as protecting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848800,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the working class. It is in practice a transfer \nto people who can already afford to invest even  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 853920,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "without the tax breaks. Now, on top of that, \nthere is the argument that a private sector  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858320,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "funded retirement is simply more efficient than a \nbloated government system without a profit motive.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863280,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the alternative is not exactly flawless \neither. Just because they have a profit motive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868240,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doesn't mean it's your profit motive. Privately \nmanaged retirement accounts currently pay out tens  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873280,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of billions of dollars every year in management \nfees to the financial institutions moving all that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877440,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "money around. An exact number is hard to find \nbecause the financial sector surrounding these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "retirement accounts is so massive. But a report \nby McKenzie estimated that roughly $84 billion  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887600,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in fee revenue could be directly attributed \nto direct contribution retirement accounts in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 892240,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2024 alone. Those are all expenses that are not \ngoing to fund anybody's retirement, let alone  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 898560,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the people that really need it. But even this is \nnot the real problem. The real problem is that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903920,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this whole arrangement was sold on one specific \npremise. It put people's financial destiny back  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909280,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in their hands. They are the ones taking the risk. \nThey are the ones investing in markets. They get  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914800,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the upside. And yes, sometimes they will have to \neat the downside. Again, to be clear, there is a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 921040,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "certain appeal to having control over your own \nfinances and not relying on an employer to pay  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925600,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you out a fixed monthly stipend. But that premise \nis uh being honored very unevenly. Because the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 932080,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fourth problem, and the one that ties this whole \nsystem together, is that the people using these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 936320,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accounts are not actually being allowed to take \nthe risk they are supposedly being rewarded for.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941360,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Every time a sector of the financial industry that \nretirement funds have heavily piled into starts  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945840,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "looking wobbly, the government steps in. Sometimes \nit's direct bailouts. Sometimes it's emergency  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 951440,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "liquidity. Sometimes it's quiet regulatory \nforbearance. Sometimes it's the Fed buying up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 956560,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an asset class that was never supposed to be its \nproblem. In every case, taxpayer resources get  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961600,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deployed to keep something propped up because \nletting it fail would hurt well people's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 966640,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "retirement and protecting Americans and their \nretirement accounts. This new bill would make  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 972400,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it easier to save for retirement. Second panel \nwill focus on protecting social security forever  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 978800,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and strengthening retirement security for all \nAmericans. That is not strictly speaking private  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 984240,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enterprise. That is a private investment account \nwith a public guarantee. quietly tacked onto the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 989600,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back end by political pressure. And the guarantee \ndoesn't only show up as bailouts. It also shows  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 995040,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up as red tape being cut, worker protections \nwatered down, consumer protections weakened,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 999760,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and antirust enforcement going quiet. All under \nthe same argument. If we make this industry play  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1005920,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fair, it might get less profitable, which might \nhurt stock prices, which might hurt 401k returns,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1011920,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which might hurt people's retirement. That \nwas an incredibly effective lobbying tool,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1017200,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is how you end up with a 20-year stretch \nwhere corporate profits hit record after record,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1021440,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wages stagnated, housing got unaffordable, health \ncare costs exploded, and every attempt to regulate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1026880,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "any of it got batted down by the same line. \nFor the vast majority of Americans, stronger  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1031680,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "worker protections, genuine consumer rights, and \na housing market that isn't an asset class first  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1036400,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would be worth enormously more than a few extra \nbasis points on a retirement account they mostly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1041520,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "don't have much in. But the basis points come \nwith the lobby. The protections do not. Now, the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1047600,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one piece of genuinely good news in all of this is \nthat America is not even close to being the worst  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1052400,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "offender. Australia, for example, has a mandatory \nretirement scheme called superanuation, where a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1057840,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "percentage of every worker's paycheck is legally \nrequired to go into a private investment account.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1062320,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that has given Australians the highest per capita \nretirement savings on the planet and also a pool  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1067360,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of private capital so enormous that the Australian \ngovernment now treats it as a kind of general  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1071680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "purpose piggy bank. If the Australian economy \nis known for one thing, it is most likely its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1076720,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "incredibly unaffordable housing market. But that \nmarket could only get so bad because Australian  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1081840,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "banks were willing to lend so much. And they were \nonly willing to lend so much because they were so  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1086160,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "well financed. And a big part of the reason why \nthey are so well financed is because Australia's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1090720,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enormous pool of retirement savings funnels a \nlot of that money into these institutions either  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1095040,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "directly through bond holdings and deposits \nor indirectly through equity. Data from the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1099440,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Australian Bureau of Statistics suggests that out \nof every dollar that gets put into these accounts,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1104160,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "27 cents gets directed towards the country's \nbanking sector, which is in turn heavily focused  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1108880,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on residential real estate mortgages. or when that \nisn't direct enough, these superanuation funds  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1114480,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have also just cut out the middleman entirely \nand started investing in single family homes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1119040,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "themselves. In plain English, a large part of \ntheir infamous housing problem is funded by and it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1124400,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is currently funding the tax advantage retirement \nof primarily wealthy investors. It sounds bad,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1131200,
